\chapter{The Device Tree, Interrupts and More Than One CPU}
\label{ch:hardware}

The kernel does not hard-code where devices are. The firmware (or qemu) passes a device tree, and \texttt{dt.c} reads it:
memory, the interrupt controller, the UART, disks, network cards, including the address translation (\texttt{ranges}) that the
Raspberry Pi's tree uses. The interrupt controller (GICv2 or GICv3, found from the tree), the generic timer and the start of the
other CPUs are the other hardware pieces this chapter describes.

@
\section{Flattened device tree reader}
\label{src:dt.c}

Reads the device tree that the boot loader (qemu) hands over and fills struct platform with the values the
kernel needs: memory, interrupt controller, console, clock, timer, CPUs, PSCI and virtio devices.
Without a device tree the defaults of the qemu ``virt'' machine are used.

