@
\section{More than one CPU}
\label{src:smp.c}

The kernel of the book was written for one CPU: its queues, tables and the scheduler have no locks.  The
processors share it with a big kernel lock (BKL):
\begin{itemize}
\item a CPU takes the lock when it enters the kernel (every exception) and gives it back when it returns to user mode or waits for an interrupt; user programs run in parallel on all CPUs;
\item [[current_task]] and [[current_as]] of the book are per CPU: they are swapped in when a CPU takes the lock and saved when it gives it back, so the code of the book sees ``its'' thread, as on one CPU;
\item the book's scheduler picks the next thread from the ready queue.  Threads that run on another CPU are in that queue, too: while the scheduler chooses, they are marked [[TSTATE_ONCPU]], which the scheduler skips (it only takes threads in state READY).  The book's [[scheduler ()]] is wrapped, see ``scheduler'' below;
\item every CPU has its own idle context (the idle thread 1 of the book is the boot CPU's);
\item a signal for a thread that runs on another CPU is handed to that CPU (an interrupt makes it enter the kernel), so that a thread is never ended or its address space freed under the feet of another CPU.
\end{itemize}
Secondary CPUs are started with PSCI ([[CPU_ON]]), as the device tree says.

